dict(sec=19, img='D_iron', cap="Photomicrograph of an endomyocardial biopsy (Prussian blue stain)",
stem="A 52-year-old man has 6 months of fatigue, exertional dyspnea and arthralgias of the second and third metacarpophalangeal joints. He was diagnosed with diabetes mellitus 1 year ago. His skin is diffusely tanned. Serum ferritin is 3,200 ng/mL and transferrin saturation is 88%. Echocardiography shows a mildly dilated left ventricle with an ejection fraction of 38%. An endomyocardial biopsy is shown.{FIG}Which of the following is the most appropriate treatment of the underlying disease?",
choices=["Deferasirox as first-line therapy","Glucocorticoids","Heart transplantation","Serial phlebotomy","Tafamidis"], key=3,
exp="Blue granules inside myocytes on <b>Prussian blue</b> are <b>iron</b>: cardiac <b>hemochromatosis</b>. Bronze skin, new diabetes, second/third metacarpophalangeal (MCP) arthropathy, very high ferritin and transferrin saturation point to <b>hereditary hemochromatosis</b> (HFE). Iron in myocytes causes a dilated (sometimes restrictive) cardiomyopathy and arrhythmias. <b>Serial phlebotomy</b> removes iron and can improve cardiac function; chelation (deferasirox, deferoxamine) is for patients who cannot be phlebotomized, such as those with transfusional overload or anemia.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Hemochromatosis causes a dilated or restrictive cardiomyopathy with Prussian blue-positive iron in myocytes; treat hereditary hemochromatosis with phlebotomy.",
wrong={0:"Chelation is for patients who cannot tolerate phlebotomy (anemia, transfusional overload).",
1:"Glucocorticoids treat cardiac sarcoidosis or myocarditis, not iron overload.",
2:"Transplantation is a last resort; removing iron can restore function.",
4:"Tafamidis stabilizes transthyretin in amyloid cardiomyopathy."},
eli5="Too much iron gets stored in the heart muscle and stiffens and weakens it. Taking blood out regularly drains the iron."),

dict(sec=19, img=None,
stem="A 48-year-old man who emigrated from rural Bolivia 20 years ago has 1 year of exertional dyspnea and palpitations. Electrocardiography shows right bundle branch block with left anterior fascicular block. Echocardiography shows a dilated left ventricle, an ejection fraction of 30% and an apical aneurysm. Coronary angiography is normal. He also reports long-standing difficulty swallowing solids and liquids.{FIG}Which of the following is the most likely cause?",
choices=["Coxsackie B virus myocarditis","Sarcoidosis","Toxoplasma gondii","Trypanosoma brucei","Trypanosoma cruzi"], key=4,
exp="A dilated cardiomyopathy with <b>right bundle branch block</b> (often with left anterior fascicular block), an <b>apical aneurysm</b> and normal coronaries, decades after living in rural Latin America, with <b>megaesophagus</b> (dysphagia from destruction of myenteric ganglia), is <b>chronic Chagas disease</b> from <b>Trypanosoma cruzi</b>, transmitted by the reduviid (\"kissing\") bug. It is now considered endemic in the United States. Acute infection may be treated with benznidazole; chronic cardiomyopathy is treated as heart failure with reduced ejection fraction (HFrEF), often with a device for conduction disease or ventricular arrhythmia.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Chagas cardiomyopathy (Trypanosoma cruzi) causes dilated cardiomyopathy with right bundle branch block (RBBB), apical aneurysm and megaesophagus or megacolon decades after exposure.",
wrong={0:"Coxsackie B myocarditis follows a viral illness and does not cause megaesophagus.",
1:"Cardiac sarcoidosis causes heart block and arrhythmias but not megaesophagus.",
2:"Toxoplasma myocarditis is rare and occurs in immunosuppressed patients.",
3:"Trypanosoma brucei causes African sleeping sickness, not cardiomyopathy with megaesophagus."},
eli5="A parasite from a bug bite in Latin America quietly damages the heart and the gut's nerves over decades. The heart gets big, the wiring blocks, and swallowing gets hard."),

dict(sec=19, img='D_hcmGross', cap="Autopsy specimen of the heart, longitudinal section through the left ventricle",
stem="A 17-year-old boy collapses and dies during a basketball game. His father died suddenly at age 35. His heart at autopsy is shown.{FIG}Which of the following best describes the inheritance and the genes most often involved?",
choices=["Autosomal dominant; desmosomal proteins","Autosomal dominant; sarcomere proteins","Autosomal recessive; frataxin","Mitochondrial; respiratory chain proteins","X-linked; dystrophin"], key=1,
exp="The septum is massively thickened (asymmetric septal hypertrophy), the cavity is small, and the arrow marks the fibrous endocardial plaque on the outflow tract where the anterior mitral leaflet strikes the septum: <b>hypertrophic cardiomyopathy</b>. It is <b>autosomal dominant</b> (about 1 in 500), from mutations in <b>sarcomere proteins</b> — <b>MYBPC3</b> (myosin-binding protein C), <b>MYH7</b> (β-myosin heavy chain), troponins I and T. It is the most common cause of sudden death in young athletes (about 36%), usually from ventricular tachycardia or fibrillation; basketball is the sport most often involved.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy is autosomal dominant, caused by sarcomere gene mutations (MYBPC3, MYH7), and is the leading cause of sudden death in young athletes.",
wrong={0:"Desmosomal gene mutations cause arrhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy.",
2:"Friedreich ataxia (frataxin, autosomal recessive) causes hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (HCM), but with ataxia and an earlier neurologic picture.",
3:"Mitochondrial cardiomyopathies are maternally inherited and rarer.",
4:"Dystrophin mutations cause a dilated cardiomyopathy with muscular dystrophy."},
eli5="A single faulty copy of a muscle-motor gene, passed from parent to child, makes the heart wall grow very thick and can cause sudden death during sport."),

