dict(sec=19, img=None,
stem="A 41-year-old man has 3 months of exertional dyspnea and edema. He was diagnosed with asthma 2 years ago. The absolute eosinophil count is 6,800/μL. Echocardiography shows normal-sized ventricles with thickened endocardium, obliteration of the left ventricular apex by thrombus, biatrial enlargement and restrictive filling.{FIG}Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
choices=["Amyloid restrictive cardiomyopathy","Constrictive pericarditis","Idiopathic dilated cardiomyopathy","Eosinophilic (Löffler) endocarditis","Obstructive hypertrophic cardiomyopathy"], key=3,
exp="Marked <b>eosinophilia</b> with a <b>restrictive cardiomyopathy</b>, thickened endocardium and <b>apical obliteration by thrombus</b> is <b>Löffler (eosinophilic) endocarditis</b>: eosinophil granule proteins damage the endocardium, causing necrosis, thrombus, then endomyocardial fibrosis. Causes include hypereosinophilic syndrome, eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis (asthma here), parasites and drugs. Treatment targets the eosinophilia (glucocorticoids) plus anticoagulation.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Löffler endocarditis is a restrictive cardiomyopathy from eosinophil-mediated endocardial damage, with apical thrombus and fibrosis.",
wrong={0:"Amyloid gives thick, speckled walls and a low-voltage ECG, not eosinophilia with apical thrombus.",
1:"Constriction thickens the pericardium, not the endocardium, and does not cause eosinophilia.",
2:"The ventricles are normal-sized with restrictive filling, not dilated.",
4:"Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (HCM) thickens the myocardium, especially the septum, without eosinophilia."},
eli5="Huge numbers of eosinophils release toxic proteins that scar the inner lining of the heart, so it can't fill and clots form at the tip."),

dict(sec=19, img=None,
stem="A 38-year-old woman has had two episodes of lightheadedness. Electrocardiography shows complete heart block with a ventricular rate of 40/min. Chest radiography shows bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy. Cardiac MRI shows patchy late gadolinium enhancement in the basal septum.{FIG}Biopsy of an affected lymph node would most likely show which of the following?",
choices=["Caseating granulomas with acid-fast bacilli","Eosinophilic infiltrates with Charcot–Leyden crystals","Monoclonal plasma cells with amyloid","Non-caseating granulomas","Reed–Sternberg cells"], key=3,
exp="A young adult with <b>complete heart block</b>, <b>bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy</b> and patchy basal septal enhancement on MRI has <b>cardiac sarcoidosis</b>: <b>non-caseating granulomas</b> infiltrate the myocardium, especially the basal septum where the conduction system runs, causing heart block, ventricular arrhythmias and a dilated or restrictive cardiomyopathy. Unexplained heart block in a patient under about 60 should prompt a search for sarcoidosis. Treatment: glucocorticoids, pacing or an implantable cardioverter-defibrillator (ICD).<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Cardiac sarcoidosis (non-caseating granulomas, often in the basal septum) causes heart block and ventricular arrhythmias in young adults.",
wrong={0:"Caseating granulomas with mycobacteria indicate tuberculosis.",
1:"Charcot–Leyden crystals suggest eosinophilic disease such as asthma or parasites.",
2:"Light-chain amyloid causes a restrictive cardiomyopathy, not hilar adenopathy with heart block.",
4:"Reed–Sternberg cells indicate Hodgkin lymphoma."},
eli5="Little clumps of inflammatory cells settle in the heart's wiring and in the chest lymph nodes. The wiring blocks, and the heart beats very slowly."),

